{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Attribution: this bug has TWO origins, and the close flag only carries one\n\nRecorded explicitly so the second does not vanish into the gap between a one-valued flag and a\ntwo-caused defect.\n\nThe ticket bundles two independent mechanisms — that is why its title has two clauses — and they\ncame from two different pieces of work. Neither is findable by `git blame`: one is an omission\n(no commit ever \"broke\" it), and the other's fix site has been correct-at-the-time since it was\nwritten. Both are attributed **by scope-of-work**, and both therefore diverge from what a\nwhole-file blame majority would name.\n\n| cause | origin | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| RC-1 — ADR-promised byte-total backstop never implemented | **`dodgy-luff-quasar` / b73c-648f-f44d-4ac5** | Its \"What\" scopes the backstop in so many words (\"backstopped by an incrementally-maintained byte total\"), yet **none of its 9 acceptance criteria mentions it**. The promise (ADR 0005 D5) and its non-implementation (`_gc_pass`) shipped in the same commit, `b25fafcd18` (#57). This is the ticket whose ACs the missing behaviour falls under. |\n| RC-2 — `materialize_tickets` never byte-accounted | **`swampy-knot-mirage` / 615c-7646-4566-4bb8** | `ec85e4cd54` introduced `materialize_tickets`, a SECOND populate path, into a store whose incremental-accounting obligation had already been established by #57. An unmet obligation, and here the *consumer* is the culprit: #57's reviewer could not have known about a future producer, whereas #57's contract was on the page in front of #67's reviewer. |\n\n**Primary, and what rides `--caused-by` on the close: `b73c-648f-f44d-4ac5`.** The ticket's title\nleads with the backstop, AC-1 is the backstop, and RC-1 is the reason the store has no\nsize-driven reclaim at all — RC-2 only makes the number that trigger reads wrong. Fix RC-2 alone\nand there is still nothing to fire; fix RC-1 alone and something fires, on a bad total. So RC-1\nis the primary defect and RC-2 is the amplifier.\n\n`615c-7646-4566-4bb8` is recorded as a second `caused_by` edge on this ticket. Both edges are\n*proven*, not guessed — which is why two are acceptable here. The usual warning against a second\nedge is about a proven edge sitting beside a **blame-derived guess**; that is not this case.\n\n**Explicitly considered and rejected: `85b4-9850-a474-4bae`** (`daabd4bee5`, \"GC the pinned\nticket-store entries (#69)\"). Its commit message asserts the tickets entries \"were invisible to\neviction AND the byte total\" and that it fixed both. It did not. It fixed `_is_entry`, which\nrestored them to `startup_sweep`'s reconciling walk, and left the incremental path untouched. It\nis a *partial* fix downstream of the omission, not its origin."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787269402650528001,"uuid":"d4e95e5d-046b-404f-bf63-d59d13fddadb"}